WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.198 --> 00:00:08.208
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:10.198
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.198 --> 00:00:13.208
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:13.218 --> 00:00:14.978
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.978 --> 00:00:17.658
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.668 --> 00:00:19.808
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.808 --> 00:00:21.668
time as improving your English.

00:00:22.128 --> 00:00:25.693
I'm Alastair Budge, and today is the

00:00:25.693 --> 00:00:29.028
third and final part of our three-part

00:00:29.038 --> 00:00:32.298
mini-series on unusual British traditions.

00:00:32.728 --> 00:00:35.578
In part one, we looked at Punch and

00:00:35.588 --> 00:00:38.888
Judy, the strange, violent puppet show.

00:00:39.398 --> 00:00:43.128
In part two, we turned to the unusual, and

00:00:43.208 --> 00:00:46.468
even more violent, history of fox hunting.

00:00:46.938 --> 00:00:50.178
And in today's episode, we step back

00:00:50.348 --> 00:00:52.678
from any single tradition to tell

00:00:52.678 --> 00:00:56.008
the story of the rise and fall of the

00:00:56.028 --> 00:00:58.818
British seaside holiday: where it came

00:00:58.818 --> 00:01:01.758
from, how it took over a century of

00:01:01.758 --> 00:01:04.498
national life, why it nearly died,

00:01:04.788 --> 00:01:07.088
and what is happening to it now.

00:01:07.878 --> 00:01:11.068
We've got a lot to get through, so let's

00:01:11.078 --> 00:01:14.678
not waste a minute and get right into it.

00:01:16.438 --> 00:01:21.268
In 1750, an English doctor named Richard

00:01:21.308 --> 00:01:24.498
Russell published a book in Latin.

00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:28.528
Its name, or at least its English name,

00:01:28.718 --> 00:01:32.388
was "A Dissertation on the Use of Sea

00:01:32.388 --> 00:01:35.688
Water in the Diseases of the Glands".

00:01:36.698 --> 00:01:39.308
In it, he argued that sea

00:01:39.338 --> 00:01:41.878
water was a powerful medicine.

00:01:42.538 --> 00:01:45.568
Both drinking it and bathing in it

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:48.398
had all sorts of magical results.

00:01:48.798 --> 00:01:50.618
It could cure glandular

00:01:50.648 --> 00:01:52.508
problems, skin conditions,

00:01:52.948 --> 00:01:56.088
tumours, even venereal disease.

00:01:56.848 --> 00:01:58.728
The book was a hit.

00:01:59.458 --> 00:02:01.508
It went through six editions.

00:02:01.778 --> 00:02:04.928
And in a roundabout way, it ended

00:02:04.928 --> 00:02:07.658
up creating an entire industry:

00:02:08.198 --> 00:02:10.578
the British seaside holiday.

00:02:11.628 --> 00:02:14.898
Now, to understand why Dr Russell's

00:02:14.898 --> 00:02:17.478
book was so important, you have

00:02:17.478 --> 00:02:19.828
to understand what came before it.

00:02:21.108 --> 00:02:24.768
In 1750, the idea of going to the

00:02:24.808 --> 00:02:27.708
coast for fun would've sounded

00:02:27.718 --> 00:02:29.818
mad to most people in Britain.

00:02:30.588 --> 00:02:33.408
The coast was where fishermen worked.

00:02:33.618 --> 00:02:36.918
It was cold, it smelled of fish, the sea

00:02:36.918 --> 00:02:39.808
was dangerous, and respectable people

00:02:40.098 --> 00:02:42.968
certainly did not go there for pleasure.

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:46.328
Russell changed that, almost

00:02:46.338 --> 00:02:48.950
single-handedly, by reframing

00:02:49.218 --> 00:02:51.228
the sea as a hospital.

00:02:52.838 --> 00:02:57.298
In 1753, he built a grand house on the

00:02:57.298 --> 00:02:59.948
seafront at a small fishing village

00:02:59.968 --> 00:03:02.949
in Sussex called Brighthelmstone.

00:03:03.818 --> 00:03:06.508
Wealthy patients began arriving to take

00:03:06.508 --> 00:03:08.788
his treatments, which involved drinking

00:03:08.788 --> 00:03:11.938
sea water, bathing in the sea, and

00:03:12.098 --> 00:03:14.858
following various complicated regimens

00:03:15.168 --> 00:03:17.998
designed to flush their systems clean.

00:03:19.588 --> 00:03:22.378
Russell's basic prescription was that

00:03:22.388 --> 00:03:25.528
the patient should bathe in cold sea

00:03:25.528 --> 00:03:28.308
water at sunrise, several times a week,

00:03:28.648 --> 00:03:30.838
for as long as they could stand it.

00:03:31.748 --> 00:03:34.868
He also recommended drinking sea water,

00:03:35.238 --> 00:03:38.078
sometimes mixed with milk, sometimes

00:03:38.228 --> 00:03:41.188
with crab's eyes or burnt sponge.

00:03:42.338 --> 00:03:45.108
To modern ears, the recipes

00:03:45.388 --> 00:03:47.088
sound almost medieval.

00:03:47.718 --> 00:03:49.608
To Russell's patients, they

00:03:49.608 --> 00:03:52.168
sounded like cutting-edge medicine.

00:03:53.008 --> 00:03:55.478
And for a generation at least,

00:03:55.878 --> 00:03:58.088
they were; people believed in them.

00:03:59.319 --> 00:04:01.758
Brighthelmstone was shortened to

00:04:01.828 --> 00:04:05.128
Brighton, and it started to grow.

00:04:05.988 --> 00:04:08.008
New lodging houses went up.

00:04:08.508 --> 00:04:10.468
Bathing machines became popular.

00:04:11.008 --> 00:04:13.378
These were small wooden huts

00:04:13.398 --> 00:04:15.728
on wheels, which were dragged

00:04:15.928 --> 00:04:17.938
into the shallows by horses.

00:04:18.998 --> 00:04:21.508
They allowed a respectable woman to

00:04:21.528 --> 00:04:24.638
change into a bathing costume, walk

00:04:24.668 --> 00:04:27.308
down a few steps directly into the sea,

00:04:27.857 --> 00:04:30.317
swim about a bit, and then come back

00:04:30.337 --> 00:04:33.118
out, all without any man seeing her

00:04:33.358 --> 00:04:35.588
in a state of undress, which of course

00:04:35.758 --> 00:04:38.048
was very important back in those days.

00:04:39.168 --> 00:04:43.748
Then in 1783, Brighton received a

00:04:43.778 --> 00:04:47.128
royal visitor: the future King George

00:04:47.128 --> 00:04:50.178
IV, who was then the Prince of Wales.

00:04:50.828 --> 00:04:54.088
Now, this young prince was something

00:04:54.118 --> 00:04:57.468
of a hedonist; he drank and ate too

00:04:57.478 --> 00:05:00.828
much, and his physicians advised the

00:05:00.888 --> 00:05:04.308
21-year-old to go to Brighton to seek

00:05:04.318 --> 00:05:06.998
the medicinal properties of the sea.

00:05:08.188 --> 00:05:09.568
He liked the place.

00:05:09.788 --> 00:05:10.888
He kept coming back.

00:05:11.288 --> 00:05:13.268
And eventually, he built a

00:05:13.288 --> 00:05:15.758
vast and slightly ridiculous

00:05:15.808 --> 00:05:17.888
palace on the seafront, which

00:05:17.888 --> 00:05:20.178
he called the Royal Pavilion.

00:05:21.218 --> 00:05:23.148
And if you've been to Brighton,

00:05:23.488 --> 00:05:25.258
you'll know it's still there today.

00:05:25.748 --> 00:05:29.058
It has domes and minarets, and

00:05:29.058 --> 00:05:31.328
looks less like an English seaside

00:05:31.328 --> 00:05:33.618
house than an Indian palace.

00:05:34.678 --> 00:05:37.388
The entire thing cost a fortune,

00:05:37.708 --> 00:05:39.858
and was the subject of plenty

00:05:39.858 --> 00:05:42.088
of mockery in the London papers.

00:05:42.748 --> 00:05:45.968
But it set the tone for Brighton.

00:05:46.998 --> 00:05:49.508
The town filled up with grand

00:05:49.688 --> 00:05:52.708
white-fronted terraces, gambling houses,

00:05:53.078 --> 00:05:55.908
theatres, and the whole apparatus of

00:05:55.908 --> 00:05:58.278
a Georgian playground for the rich.

00:05:59.488 --> 00:06:03.710
By the time George IV died in 1830,

00:06:04.018 --> 00:06:06.748
Brighton had a population of more

00:06:06.778 --> 00:06:09.358
than forty thousand people, more

00:06:09.358 --> 00:06:11.698
than ten times what it had been

00:06:11.908 --> 00:06:13.878
when Dr Russell first arrived.

00:06:15.098 --> 00:06:18.358
Other resorts soon followed, or grew

00:06:18.358 --> 00:06:21.438
in parallel: Scarborough, Margate

00:06:21.758 --> 00:06:23.868
and Weymouth on the coast, as well

00:06:23.868 --> 00:06:27.098
as inland spa towns such as Bath.

00:06:28.018 --> 00:06:30.238
The whole apparatus of the early

00:06:30.248 --> 00:06:32.698
British seaside was in place by

00:06:32.698 --> 00:06:34.188
the early nineteenth century.

00:06:34.508 --> 00:06:36.098
The lodging houses.

00:06:36.098 --> 00:06:37.428
The pump rooms.

00:06:37.438 --> 00:06:39.408
The seafront promenades.

00:06:39.478 --> 00:06:42.578
The polite, well-dressed crowds taking

00:06:42.578 --> 00:06:44.418
their morning walk along the front.

00:06:45.428 --> 00:06:48.278
But at this point, it was still a

00:06:48.278 --> 00:06:51.828
pursuit of the wealthy, the aristocracy.

00:06:52.728 --> 00:06:55.978
A trip to the seaside took several days.

00:06:56.278 --> 00:06:58.788
It wasn't exactly compatible with a job.

00:06:59.368 --> 00:07:01.338
And it cost serious money.

00:07:02.668 --> 00:07:04.678
The vast majority of people in Britain

00:07:05.118 --> 00:07:07.308
had never been near the coast in their

00:07:07.318 --> 00:07:09.508
lives, not for pleasure at least.

00:07:10.888 --> 00:07:12.378
The thing that would change

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:14.808
all of that was a new piece

00:07:14.838 --> 00:07:17.488
of technology: the railway.

00:07:19.008 --> 00:07:21.408
Railways arrived in Britain in the

00:07:21.448 --> 00:07:24.878
1830s, and within a generation,

00:07:25.248 --> 00:07:27.048
they had remade the country.

00:07:28.438 --> 00:07:32.798
For our purposes, the key year is 1846,

00:07:33.078 --> 00:07:35.598
when the railway reached Blackpool, on

00:07:35.598 --> 00:07:37.658
the north west coast of England, about

00:07:37.658 --> 00:07:39.618
fifty kilometres north of Liverpool.

00:07:41.248 --> 00:07:44.468
Until then, Blackpool was a small

00:07:44.568 --> 00:07:46.658
Lancashire village on a windy

00:07:46.658 --> 00:07:48.698
stretch of the Irish Sea coast,

00:07:49.048 --> 00:07:51.698
of no great importance to anybody.

00:07:53.128 --> 00:07:56.428
Within a few decades, it would be the most

00:07:56.498 --> 00:07:59.448
visited seaside resort in the country.

00:08:00.858 --> 00:08:03.168
The reason was geography.

00:08:04.195 --> 00:08:06.669
In the 19th century, the north

00:08:06.669 --> 00:08:09.069
of England was the great economic

00:08:09.109 --> 00:08:10.459
powerhouse of the country.

00:08:11.299 --> 00:08:14.359
And Blackpool sat within easy train

00:08:14.359 --> 00:08:16.759
reach of the great industrial towns of

00:08:16.759 --> 00:08:19.579
the area, like Manchester, Liverpool,

00:08:19.889 --> 00:08:22.589
Preston, Bolton, and Bradford.

00:08:23.629 --> 00:08:26.449
These were the mill towns, the cotton

00:08:26.479 --> 00:08:28.939
and wool towns, where hundreds of

00:08:28.949 --> 00:08:31.439
thousands of people put in long,

00:08:31.659 --> 00:08:33.729
hard, weeks in the factories.

00:08:34.929 --> 00:08:37.089
When the railway first arrived in

00:08:37.109 --> 00:08:39.729
Blackpool, the visitors were still

00:08:39.739 --> 00:08:42.409
mainly the "respectable" middle classes.

00:08:42.809 --> 00:08:45.019
And for them, a trip to the seaside

00:08:45.209 --> 00:08:47.769
was still the genteel, slightly

00:08:47.769 --> 00:08:50.159
medicinal sort of activity that

00:08:50.159 --> 00:08:51.809
you heard about a few minutes ago.

00:08:52.739 --> 00:08:56.369
And if you were a seaside town trying

00:08:56.369 --> 00:08:58.699
to attract these respectable visitors,

00:08:59.049 --> 00:09:01.979
there was one thing you needed: a pier.

00:09:02.909 --> 00:09:05.249
A pier, if you aren't familiar with the

00:09:05.249 --> 00:09:08.499
word, is a long raised platform that

00:09:08.509 --> 00:09:11.459
stretches out from the beach into the sea.

00:09:13.119 --> 00:09:15.259
Blackpool's first pier, the

00:09:15.449 --> 00:09:18.899
North Pier, opened in 1863.

00:09:19.909 --> 00:09:22.619
It was a quiet promenade, somewhere

00:09:22.619 --> 00:09:25.249
to stroll, take the bracing sea

00:09:25.249 --> 00:09:28.199
air, and exchange polite nods

00:09:28.319 --> 00:09:30.449
with other well-dressed visitors.

00:09:31.399 --> 00:09:33.889
But over the following decades,

00:09:34.229 --> 00:09:37.159
wages rose, train fares fell,

00:09:37.519 --> 00:09:39.859
and the factory workers began to

00:09:39.859 --> 00:09:41.869
arrive in far greater numbers.

00:09:43.029 --> 00:09:46.219
Many came during Wakes Weeks, when

00:09:46.439 --> 00:09:48.889
the mills in a particular town shut

00:09:48.889 --> 00:09:51.929
down and workers had a rare week off.

00:09:52.579 --> 00:09:55.329
Suddenly, a cheap train could take

00:09:55.339 --> 00:09:57.499
them from the factory floor to the

00:09:57.499 --> 00:09:59.859
beach in just a couple of hours.

00:10:00.649 --> 00:10:03.269
But this new crowd wanted

00:10:03.299 --> 00:10:05.039
a different kind of pier.

00:10:06.239 --> 00:10:08.429
Just five years after the North

00:10:08.429 --> 00:10:11.629
Pier, in 1868, a second pier

00:10:11.909 --> 00:10:14.369
opened a short walk down the front.

00:10:15.269 --> 00:10:18.189
This was the Central Pier, and it

00:10:18.189 --> 00:10:20.169
was a different beast entirely:

00:10:21.079 --> 00:10:24.399
dancing, music, drinking, and cheap

00:10:24.449 --> 00:10:27.319
entertainment, all aimed squarely

00:10:27.519 --> 00:10:29.999
at the new, working-class crowds.

00:10:31.109 --> 00:10:33.519
The two piers stood within

00:10:33.519 --> 00:10:34.479
sight of each other.

00:10:34.999 --> 00:10:37.889
Both were open to anyone, but

00:10:38.199 --> 00:10:40.509
every visitor to Blackpool knew

00:10:40.519 --> 00:10:42.319
which pier was meant for them.

00:10:43.719 --> 00:10:46.399
And it was the working classes who

00:10:46.449 --> 00:10:48.669
quickly became the dominant force.

00:10:49.329 --> 00:10:52.139
By the 1880s, Blackpool was

00:10:52.139 --> 00:10:54.219
getting hundreds of thousands of

00:10:54.219 --> 00:10:56.229
working-class visitors every summer.

00:10:57.599 --> 00:11:00.739
And this format of "cheap and cheerful",

00:11:00.989 --> 00:11:03.479
transforming the seaside holiday into

00:11:03.479 --> 00:11:05.289
something accessible to everyone in

00:11:05.289 --> 00:11:08.199
society, was incredibly successful.

00:11:09.329 --> 00:11:10.989
Brighton followed suit.

00:11:11.409 --> 00:11:13.609
So did Margate, and practically

00:11:13.659 --> 00:11:15.229
every other seaside town.

00:11:16.159 --> 00:11:19.269
By the late Victorian period, the seaside

00:11:19.329 --> 00:11:21.459
was no longer just for the wealthy.

00:11:21.729 --> 00:11:23.399
It was for everybody.

00:11:24.509 --> 00:11:26.619
As for what you actually did there,

00:11:27.099 --> 00:11:28.979
a Victorian day at the seaside

00:11:29.329 --> 00:11:31.519
had a recognisable rhythm to it.

00:11:32.039 --> 00:11:34.999
You arrived on a morning train, found

00:11:34.999 --> 00:11:36.569
your boarding house, dropped your

00:11:36.579 --> 00:11:39.049
bags, and walked down to the front.

00:11:39.609 --> 00:11:41.169
You took your shoes off and

00:11:41.209 --> 00:11:42.663
paddled in the shallows.

00:11:43.009 --> 00:11:45.371
You ate fish and chips out of a newspaper.

00:11:46.039 --> 00:11:47.089
You watched a Punch and

00:11:47.089 --> 00:11:48.879
Judy show on the sand.

00:11:49.189 --> 00:11:51.539
You queued up for the donkey rides.

00:11:51.989 --> 00:11:54.549
In the afternoon, you walked along the

00:11:54.559 --> 00:11:57.719
pier, and perhaps paid a penny to look at

00:11:57.739 --> 00:12:00.369
the strange wonders in a peep-show booth.

00:12:01.459 --> 00:12:03.659
In the evening, there was a music hall

00:12:03.689 --> 00:12:06.209
performance, or a brass band on the

00:12:06.209 --> 00:12:08.999
bandstand, or a singalong in the pub.

00:12:10.009 --> 00:12:13.069
By the time you fell into bed, sunburned

00:12:13.249 --> 00:12:15.579
and full of beer, you had had the kind

00:12:15.579 --> 00:12:17.749
of holiday your great-grandparents

00:12:17.989 --> 00:12:19.739
could not even have imagined.

00:12:21.539 --> 00:12:23.639
Alongside the railways, there were

00:12:23.699 --> 00:12:26.049
two more things that made it work.

00:12:27.289 --> 00:12:29.649
The first was the Bank Holidays

00:12:29.689 --> 00:12:33.829
Act of 1871, which created the very

00:12:33.829 --> 00:12:36.249
idea of the modern public holiday.

00:12:37.909 --> 00:12:40.449
Before this, ordinary workers

00:12:40.649 --> 00:12:42.909
had almost no guaranteed holiday;

00:12:43.199 --> 00:12:45.659
they were at the whim of a kind

00:12:45.669 --> 00:12:47.699
boss letting them have a day off.

00:12:48.999 --> 00:12:52.359
After 1871, they had four official

00:12:52.379 --> 00:12:54.809
bank holidays a year, in addition

00:12:55.069 --> 00:12:57.189
to Christmas and Good Friday.

00:12:58.619 --> 00:13:01.619
And for hundreds of thousands of people,

00:13:02.149 --> 00:13:04.719
every one of those bank holidays became

00:13:04.749 --> 00:13:07.959
an excuse to get on a train to the coast.

00:13:09.249 --> 00:13:11.109
The second thing that made it work

00:13:11.359 --> 00:13:14.449
was an entire ecosystem of seaside

00:13:14.469 --> 00:13:17.369
entertainment, much of it imported

00:13:17.369 --> 00:13:20.609
from elsewhere and rebadged as British.

00:13:21.339 --> 00:13:23.728
There were donkey rides for the children.

00:13:24.089 --> 00:13:25.899
There were brass bands on the pier.

00:13:26.459 --> 00:13:29.129
There were saucy postcards, those

00:13:29.309 --> 00:13:31.919
rude, comic, cartoon-style cards

00:13:32.149 --> 00:13:34.379
that became their own minor art form.

00:13:34.959 --> 00:13:37.429
There was fish and chips, which was

00:13:37.429 --> 00:13:40.388
itself a fusion of fried fish brought to

00:13:40.388 --> 00:13:42.838
Britain by Jewish immigrants and chipped

00:13:42.838 --> 00:13:45.308
potatoes from somewhere on the Continent.

00:13:45.908 --> 00:13:47.428
And there was Punch and Judy.

00:13:48.068 --> 00:13:50.378
As you will remember from part one of

00:13:50.378 --> 00:13:53.338
this mini-series, Mr Punch had started

00:13:53.338 --> 00:13:56.188
life as an Italian street puppet character

00:13:56.538 --> 00:13:59.408
before finding his calling, his perfect

00:13:59.428 --> 00:14:02.188
English home, on a windy British beach.

00:14:03.258 --> 00:14:07.888
And from the 1880s to the 1950s,

00:14:07.978 --> 00:14:11.528
for about seventy years, this was

00:14:11.528 --> 00:14:13.988
the centre of British leisure.

00:14:14.728 --> 00:14:17.408
If you lived in a city, the seaside

00:14:17.588 --> 00:14:19.518
was where you went to escape.

00:14:20.338 --> 00:14:22.878
If you ran a hotel or a boarding

00:14:22.878 --> 00:14:25.768
house in Brighton or Blackpool or

00:14:25.768 --> 00:14:28.998
Skegness, you ran a thriving business,

00:14:29.188 --> 00:14:31.358
with a guaranteed flow of customers.

00:14:32.268 --> 00:14:34.838
If you were a child, summer meant

00:14:34.848 --> 00:14:37.478
sand in your sandwiches, salt in

00:14:37.478 --> 00:14:40.128
your hair, and a sunburned nose.

00:14:40.808 --> 00:14:43.088
It seemed like the British seaside

00:14:43.098 --> 00:14:45.448
holiday was a permanent fixture

00:14:45.688 --> 00:14:48.188
of life in Blighty, in Britain.

00:14:49.158 --> 00:14:52.538
And as paid holidays became more common,

00:14:52.918 --> 00:14:55.928
the seaside stopped being just a day trip.

00:14:56.168 --> 00:14:58.368
It became a full week away.

00:14:59.488 --> 00:15:03.138
In 1936, a young entrepreneur named

00:15:03.158 --> 00:15:05.768
Billy Butlin capitalised on this,

00:15:06.058 --> 00:15:08.858
opening a new kind of seaside resort at

00:15:08.858 --> 00:15:11.418
Skegness, on the east coast of England.

00:15:12.068 --> 00:15:14.268
He called it a holiday camp.

00:15:15.758 --> 00:15:17.578
The idea was simple.

00:15:18.148 --> 00:15:21.178
For one fixed low price per week,

00:15:21.438 --> 00:15:23.678
a family could get accommodation,

00:15:24.038 --> 00:15:26.538
three meals a day, and constant

00:15:26.578 --> 00:15:29.108
entertainment, all in one place.

00:15:29.698 --> 00:15:30.528
Dance halls.

00:15:30.848 --> 00:15:31.768
Swimming pools.

00:15:32.048 --> 00:15:33.178
Sports fields.

00:15:33.248 --> 00:15:34.058
Singalongs.

00:15:34.228 --> 00:15:35.538
Children's activities.

00:15:35.738 --> 00:15:37.368
Everything organised, everything

00:15:37.378 --> 00:15:40.618
included, no nasty surprises on the bill.

00:15:42.308 --> 00:15:44.358
The first Butlin's camp at

00:15:44.358 --> 00:15:47.358
Skegness was a phenomenal success.

00:15:48.118 --> 00:15:50.388
The opening week alone brought

00:15:50.508 --> 00:15:52.708
ten thousand booking requests.

00:15:53.888 --> 00:15:55.108
After the Second World War,

00:15:55.548 --> 00:15:57.758
Butlin's expanded, opening new

00:15:57.778 --> 00:15:59.678
camps up and down the country.

00:16:00.208 --> 00:16:02.288
And a handful of rivals

00:16:02.578 --> 00:16:04.308
copied the winning formula.

00:16:05.338 --> 00:16:07.648
This was the absolute peak

00:16:07.938 --> 00:16:09.458
of the British seaside.

00:16:10.398 --> 00:16:12.908
But within a generation, it

00:16:12.908 --> 00:16:14.998
would have all but disappeared.

00:16:16.108 --> 00:16:18.458
The killer was the package holiday.

00:16:19.258 --> 00:16:22.668
From the late 1950s onwards, cheap

00:16:22.738 --> 00:16:24.988
charter flights began carrying

00:16:25.018 --> 00:16:26.708
British holidaymakers to the

00:16:26.708 --> 00:16:29.038
Mediterranean, principally to Spain.

00:16:30.008 --> 00:16:32.768
The Costa del Sol, the Costa Brava,

00:16:33.118 --> 00:16:35.648
the Balearic Islands: all of them

00:16:35.808 --> 00:16:38.448
were suddenly accessible to ordinary

00:16:38.448 --> 00:16:40.818
British people for not much more

00:16:41.048 --> 00:16:43.143
than the cost of a week at Butlin's.

00:16:44.268 --> 00:16:46.648
And what they offered was something

00:16:46.658 --> 00:16:49.998
Britain could not match: reliable,

00:16:50.388 --> 00:16:52.928
practically guaranteed sunshine.

00:16:54.687 --> 00:16:56.088
It's hard to overstate

00:16:56.448 --> 00:16:57.968
how decisive this was.

00:16:58.758 --> 00:17:01.148
The British seaside had built its

00:17:01.148 --> 00:17:03.698
entire offer around the idea that

00:17:03.778 --> 00:17:06.298
ordinary people could escape the city,

00:17:06.718 --> 00:17:09.978
breathe sea air, and have a good time,

00:17:10.528 --> 00:17:12.978
and the weather almost didn't matter.

00:17:13.488 --> 00:17:15.188
It might rain, sure, but

00:17:15.448 --> 00:17:16.648
you'd have a lot of fun.

00:17:17.948 --> 00:17:20.668
Spain offered something different.

00:17:21.298 --> 00:17:24.148
Or rather, it offered the same thing, but

00:17:24.388 --> 00:17:27.358
minus the rain, wind and black clouds.

00:17:28.018 --> 00:17:31.198
Instead, you'd get glorious sun, and

00:17:31.488 --> 00:17:33.808
sea water that was actually pleasant

00:17:33.828 --> 00:17:35.928
to be in, and didn't leave your

00:17:35.958 --> 00:17:38.018
teeth chattering and your fingers

00:17:38.018 --> 00:17:40.218
blue if you stayed in it too long.

00:17:40.618 --> 00:17:42.268
There were other factors too,

00:17:42.478 --> 00:17:44.858
other non-sun-related factors.

00:17:45.688 --> 00:17:47.788
The post-war British holidaymaker

00:17:48.088 --> 00:17:50.018
was richer than ever before.

00:17:51.128 --> 00:17:53.898
New package holiday companies started

00:17:53.898 --> 00:17:56.048
up to do the planning for you.

00:17:56.708 --> 00:17:59.158
They handled the flight, the airport

00:17:59.158 --> 00:18:01.988
transfer, the hotel, and the meals.

00:18:02.458 --> 00:18:04.538
You did not need to speak any Spanish.

00:18:04.858 --> 00:18:06.718
You did not need to organise anything.

00:18:07.065 --> 00:18:09.315
You handed over your British pounds

00:18:09.335 --> 00:18:11.925
in Britain, got on a plane in

00:18:11.935 --> 00:18:14.365
Britain, and woke up the next morning

00:18:14.575 --> 00:18:16.685
next to a Mediterranean beach.

00:18:17.285 --> 00:18:19.075
You could probably even start

00:18:19.115 --> 00:18:21.075
your day off with a full English

00:18:21.075 --> 00:18:23.855
breakfast and a pint of English beer.

00:18:24.655 --> 00:18:28.705
Given all this, by the 1970s and

00:18:28.705 --> 00:18:31.745
1980s, the great British seaside

00:18:31.755 --> 00:18:34.265
resorts were in deep trouble.

00:18:35.085 --> 00:18:36.735
Boarding houses closed.

00:18:37.175 --> 00:18:39.775
Piers fell into disrepair.

00:18:40.335 --> 00:18:43.485
Towns that had thrived on summer visitors

00:18:43.835 --> 00:18:46.375
found their high streets boarded up.

00:18:47.185 --> 00:18:50.505
Some resorts pivoted into cheap hotels

00:18:50.505 --> 00:18:52.815
and low-cost temporary accommodation,

00:18:53.195 --> 00:18:55.205
for example for homeless people.

00:18:56.415 --> 00:18:59.565
And these towns experienced very

00:18:59.565 --> 00:19:02.305
high levels of economic deprivation.

00:19:03.205 --> 00:19:06.625
By the early 2000s, Margate had

00:19:06.635 --> 00:19:08.425
one of the highest unemployment

00:19:08.425 --> 00:19:10.175
rates in the south of England.

00:19:10.885 --> 00:19:12.895
This was a town that had once

00:19:12.915 --> 00:19:14.885
been one of the most popular

00:19:14.885 --> 00:19:16.805
resorts on the south coast.

00:19:17.585 --> 00:19:20.545
Blackpool had become a place stand-up

00:19:20.575 --> 00:19:23.025
comedians would poke fun at, the

00:19:23.025 --> 00:19:25.985
home of cheap stag and hen parties.

00:19:27.115 --> 00:19:30.135
In cultural terms, the seaside was

00:19:30.135 --> 00:19:33.455
somewhere between a sad memory and a joke.

00:19:34.145 --> 00:19:36.855
But then, gradually, something

00:19:36.875 --> 00:19:38.325
else began to happen.

00:19:39.325 --> 00:19:42.135
Things started to get better, or

00:19:42.135 --> 00:19:44.125
at least, a new trend appeared.

00:19:45.645 --> 00:19:48.755
Starting with Margate, many of these

00:19:48.765 --> 00:19:51.465
former seaside towns started to

00:19:51.475 --> 00:19:54.414
reinvent themselves as new artistic

00:19:54.414 --> 00:19:56.611
centres for creatives who had been

00:19:56.651 --> 00:19:59.271
pushed out of London by high prices.

00:19:59.951 --> 00:20:01.931
A new wave of restaurants,

00:20:02.191 --> 00:20:04.691
bars, and design shops followed.

00:20:05.244 --> 00:20:08.466
Margate, and other seaside towns like

00:20:08.466 --> 00:20:11.366
it, started to be talked about as a

00:20:11.376 --> 00:20:14.166
fashionable place to spend a weekend.

00:20:14.906 --> 00:20:17.186
These are places you can still get to

00:20:17.216 --> 00:20:20.176
pretty easily, by train or by car from

00:20:20.176 --> 00:20:22.906
London, and they now have boutique

00:20:22.906 --> 00:20:25.806
hotels, coffee shops, and restaurants.

00:20:27.066 --> 00:20:29.556
Now, this is not the case in every

00:20:29.556 --> 00:20:32.646
seaside town, and many British seaside

00:20:32.646 --> 00:20:35.156
towns remain in serious trouble.

00:20:36.056 --> 00:20:37.796
The towns that have recovered

00:20:38.056 --> 00:20:39.446
have come back mainly because

00:20:39.446 --> 00:20:40.756
they are close enough to London.

00:20:41.316 --> 00:20:42.636
In other parts of the country,

00:20:42.916 --> 00:20:44.816
the picture is much bleaker.

00:20:45.746 --> 00:20:48.736
Towns like Blackpool and Skegness have

00:20:48.736 --> 00:20:51.596
struggled for decades to find a new

00:20:51.696 --> 00:20:54.396
economic identity, and have become

00:20:54.396 --> 00:20:56.906
known for high levels of unemployment,

00:20:57.256 --> 00:21:00.226
deprivation, and political disenchantment,

00:21:00.456 --> 00:21:02.646
with politicians starting to talk about

00:21:02.646 --> 00:21:05.176
the so-called "seaside town problem".

00:21:06.716 --> 00:21:09.786
And even in the success stories,

00:21:10.116 --> 00:21:12.036
not everyone is delighted.

00:21:13.056 --> 00:21:15.656
Long-time residents have watched local

00:21:15.656 --> 00:21:18.966
cafés become artisan bakeries, their

00:21:18.966 --> 00:21:21.306
favourite pubs become hipster wine

00:21:21.306 --> 00:21:24.926
bars, and their rent double in a decade.

00:21:25.736 --> 00:21:28.366
Sure, it's good news if you own a

00:21:28.376 --> 00:21:31.306
hotel in Margate, but for everyone

00:21:31.336 --> 00:21:34.106
not catering to the well-heeled London

00:21:34.136 --> 00:21:36.816
tourist, it's turning your town into

00:21:36.816 --> 00:21:38.666
something that feels very different.

00:21:39.806 --> 00:21:43.266
Still, the tourists are coming back, and

00:21:43.496 --> 00:21:45.166
they are bringing their money with them.

00:21:45.826 --> 00:21:48.116
People are once again going to

00:21:48.116 --> 00:21:49.926
Margate for the weekend, for

00:21:49.926 --> 00:21:52.216
the first time in fifty years.

00:21:53.436 --> 00:21:55.896
And just as in the Victorian

00:21:55.926 --> 00:21:58.476
era, a modern weekend in Margate

00:21:58.826 --> 00:22:01.056
has its own recognisable rhythm.

00:22:01.976 --> 00:22:04.156
You arrive on a Friday evening

00:22:04.356 --> 00:22:06.086
on the fast train from London.

00:22:06.626 --> 00:22:08.526
You drop your bag at a small

00:22:08.526 --> 00:22:11.426
hotel run by a former London chef.

00:22:12.266 --> 00:22:14.056
You eat at a restaurant where the

00:22:14.056 --> 00:22:16.656
menu is short and the staff have

00:22:16.666 --> 00:22:19.106
well-groomed beards and tattoos.

00:22:19.726 --> 00:22:22.276
You walk along the seafront on Saturday

00:22:22.276 --> 00:22:24.786
morning, past the same Victorian

00:22:24.786 --> 00:22:26.496
buildings that have been there

00:22:26.646 --> 00:22:29.036
for a hundred and fifty years, and

00:22:29.256 --> 00:22:31.656
visit the contemporary art gallery.

00:22:32.306 --> 00:22:34.386
You buy a coffee from a place

00:22:34.536 --> 00:22:36.086
that roasts its own beans.

00:22:36.956 --> 00:22:39.086
It costs as much as a coffee in central

00:22:39.086 --> 00:22:42.216
London, but on your City salary, you

00:22:42.216 --> 00:22:44.116
don't worry too much about the price.

00:22:45.116 --> 00:22:47.676
So, to wrap up this mini-series,

00:22:47.956 --> 00:22:50.306
like Punch and Judy, and like the

00:22:50.306 --> 00:22:52.916
fox hunt, the British seaside has

00:22:52.916 --> 00:22:54.766
been many different things over

00:22:54.796 --> 00:22:56.816
its three centuries of existence.

00:22:57.436 --> 00:22:59.916
Each generation has remade it.

00:23:00.236 --> 00:23:02.986
Each remaking has lasted for a while,

00:23:03.086 --> 00:23:05.566
and then given way to something else.

00:23:06.366 --> 00:23:08.026
What the next remaking will

00:23:08.026 --> 00:23:09.746
look like, we will see.

00:23:10.116 --> 00:23:12.076
But it seems we are not quite

00:23:12.076 --> 00:23:14.246
finished with our seaside yet.

00:23:16.146 --> 00:23:18.806
OK, then, this is it for today's

00:23:18.816 --> 00:23:20.536
episode on the rise and fall of the

00:23:20.546 --> 00:23:22.756
British seaside holiday, and the final

00:23:22.756 --> 00:23:24.816
part of our three-part mini-series

00:23:25.016 --> 00:23:27.056
on unusual British traditions.

00:23:27.396 --> 00:23:28.786
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:23:29.096 --> 00:23:31.036
and that you've learnt something new.

00:23:31.636 --> 00:23:33.246
As always, I would love to know

00:23:33.256 --> 00:23:34.606
what you thought of this episode,

00:23:34.876 --> 00:23:36.545
and of the whole mini-series.

00:23:37.026 --> 00:23:38.276
Have you ever been on a

00:23:38.316 --> 00:23:40.036
trip to the British seaside?

00:23:40.436 --> 00:23:42.786
Where did you go, and what did you think?

00:23:43.216 --> 00:23:44.786
I'd love to know, so let's

00:23:44.786 --> 00:23:46.226
get this discussion started.

00:23:46.506 --> 00:23:47.696
You can head right into our

00:23:47.696 --> 00:23:49.516
community forum, which is at

00:23:49.516 --> 00:23:52.276
community.leonardoenglish.com and get

00:23:52.276 --> 00:23:54.596
chatting away to other curious minds.

00:23:55.426 --> 00:23:57.366
You've been listening to English Learning

00:23:57.366 --> 00:24:00.246
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:24:00.666 --> 00:24:03.856
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:24:03.906 --> 00:24:06.876
I'll catch you in the next episode.

